  "best_model_id": "PGS005170",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match dementia models are present (e.g., PGS000929/PGS000946/PGS001827/PGS002035/PGS004281-84/PGS004318/PGS005170). PGS005170 is best supported because it has strong phenotype alignment (phenotyping_reported: incident all-cause dementia; trait_efo: dementia) and a method with favorable structural properties (explicit integrative/aggregated PRS across multiple component PRSs; 1,320,229 variants), which domain knowledge indicates is strongly preferred over single-score methods when available. It also provides a comparable discrimination metric (AUROC=0.761) and an effect size (HR=1.25) under largely demographic/PC/APOE covariates, without obvious risk-calculator packaging. Main limitation: the evidence does not include PGS-only (no-covariates) metrics, and there are other exact-label dementia candidates with explicit PGS-only AUROC/R2 (notably the UKB sparse snpnet models PGS000929/PGS000946), creating some residual uncertainty about standalone PRS contribution despite domain guidance to down-weight pan-trait framework scores."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004163",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match rheumatoid arthritis candidates are present (multiple models with trait_efo=rheumatoid arthritis). Among them, PGS004163 is best supported by the visible evidence because it has strong phenotype alignment (phenotyping_reported: \"Seropositive RA\"), uses a multi-score aggregation approach (method_name: \"UKBB-EUR.MultiPRS.CV\"), and shows the strongest reported discrimination in this coherent RA benchmarking family (full-model AUROC 0.7469 with R2 0.1376; effect size OR 2.46) compared with sibling RA models from the same publication context (e.g., LDpred2.CV AUROC 0.7283; lassosum.CV AUROC 0.7110). Main limitation: the selected metrics appear to be full-model (covariates listed as 0 but the metric is labeled full_model_auc), and this family’s endpoint is specifically seropositive RA rather than all RA; additionally, other direct RA models exist (e.g., PGS002745, PGS002769) but lack comparable PRS-only metrics here, lowering certainty in cross-study comparability."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004163",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates for rheumatoid arthritis are present (multiple models with trait_efo=rheumatoid arthritis). Among them, PGS004163 is best supported by the visible evidence because it has strong endpoint alignment (phenotyping_reported: \"Seropositive RA\"; trait_efo: rheumatoid arthritis), uses an explicit multi-score aggregation method (UKBB-EUR.MultiPRS.CV), and shows the strongest reported discrimination within the same publication family/endpoints shown here (full-model AUROC 0.7469; R² 0.1376; OR 2.46) compared with other 2023-12-19 RA siblings (e.g., LDpred2.CV AUROC 0.7283; lassosum.CV AUROC 0.7110). Main limitation: the reported metrics appear to be full-model (covariates listed as \"0\"), and many other direct-match RA candidates exist in the context (e.g., PGS002745, PGS002769, PGS001310/PGS001311, PGS004873) with differing endpoints (incident RA, time-to-event, serostatus) and heterogeneous metric reporting, making cross-study comparability incomplete; therefore evidence supports a preference but not a clearly definitive best-in-class selection."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002092",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All three candidates are direct matches for dupuytren contracture (trait_efo = Dupuytren Contracture; phenotyping_reported explicitly Dupuytren’s disease/contracture). PGS001254 is from a pan-trait sparse UKB framework (“Significant sparse polygenic risk scores across 813 traits…”) with only 11 variants and an L1/sparse-style approach (snpnet), which domain guidance flags as prone to weaker external generalization despite having reported AUROC/R² fields. PGS001880 and PGS002092 are sibling scores from the same portability study with the same endpoint and covariates; between them, PGS002092 uses a genome-wide shrinkage method (LDpred2) with far more variants (484,484 vs 2,443) and slightly higher reported partial-r (0.0797 vs 0.0783), which together are supportive structural/performance signals. However, both portability-study models lack PRS-comparable AUROC/R² in the provided metadata (only partial correlation is available), leaving limited comparable discrimination evidence and keeping confidence below high."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002037",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Both candidates are direct matches to the target trait (trait_efo = nicotine dependence; trait_reported/phenotyping_reported = Tobacco use disorder) and come from the same UKB portability publication with the same training cohort (UKB), similar covariates (basic demographics + PCs), and the same stated validation sample size (n=19,400), so endpoint fidelity and study context do not differentiate them. PGS002037 is better supported on the visible evidence because it shows higher reported PRS-comparable performance (partial correlation 0.0741 [0.0601, 0.0881] vs 0.0557 [0.0416, 0.0697] for PGS001830) and uses a more genome-wide shrinkage-style method with far more variants (LDpred2; 847,691 variants) than the penalized regression score (13,838 variants), which is generally a positive structural signal when endpoints are otherwise aligned. However, confidence is limited because neither model reports standard PRS-only AUROC or R2, and both are from a portability/framework-style UKB study rather than a clearly disease-focused multi-cohort nicotine-dependence effort, so the evidence base for a clearly strong recommendation remains incomplete."
}
